\section{Method}\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Preliminaries}\label{sec:background}

\paragraph{Goal Accessibility Ratio.}
We denote the task specification tokens in the system prompt as \emph{goal tokens} $\mathcal{G}$. \citet{dongre2026attention} defined the Goal Accessibility Ratio (GAR) for a model with $L$ layers and $H$ heads at turn $\tau$ as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gar}
  \text{GAR}(\tau) = \frac{1}{L \cdot H \cdot |\mathcal{R}_\tau|} \sum_{\ell=1}^{L} \sum_{h=1}^{H} \sum_{i \in \mathcal{R}_\tau} \sum_{j \in \mathcal{G}} A_{i,j}^{(\ell,h)},
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{R}_\tau$ is the set of response tokens at turn $\tau$ and $A_{i,j}^{(\ell,h)}$ is the attention weight from token $i$ to token $j$ at layer $\ell$, head $h$.

\paragraph{Two GAR variants.}
\textbf{GAR-Dongre} is the original definition (Eq.~\ref{eq:gar}), averaging over all response tokens. \textbf{GAR-ours} restricts the measurement to the \emph{last token} of each assistant turn:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gar-ours}
  \text{GAR\text{-}ours}(\tau) = \frac{1}{L \cdot H} \sum_{\ell=1}^{L} \sum_{h=1}^{H} \sum_{j \in \mathcal{G}} A_{t_\tau,\, j}^{(\ell,h)},
\end{equation}
where $t_\tau$ is the last token index at turn $\tau$. Attention at this position reflects the information drawn upon for continuation; we use GAR-ours as the optimization target for $\mathcal{L}_\text{GAR}$ (\S\ref{sec:loss}).

\subsection{ThreadGate Architecture}

ThreadGate adds a learned gating mechanism to the transformer's residual stream. At each layer $l$, the residual update is modulated by a scalar gate:
\begin{equation}
  x_{l+1} = x_l + g_l \cdot f_l(x_l),
\end{equation}
where $f_l$ denotes the sublayer computation (self-attention or FFN). This resembles ReZero \citep{bachlechner2021rezero}, which initializes a single trainable scalar at zero to stabilize deep network training. ThreadGate differs in three ways: (i)~gates are \emph{content-conditioned}---a function of the hidden state, not a fixed scalar; (ii)~gates are \emph{turn-conditioned}---incorporating conversation-position features; (iii)~gates are initialized near identity ($\sigma(1.5) \approx 0.82$) rather than zero, preserving the pretrained model's computation.

\paragraph{Gate network.} Each gate $g_l$ is produced by a two-layer MLP:
\begin{align}\label{eq:gate}
  g_l = \sigma\bigl(W_2\,\text{ReLU}(&W_1\,[\text{proj}(x_l);\;\text{emb}(t_d, t_i)] \notag\\
  &+ b_1) + b_2\bigr),
\end{align}
where $\text{proj}(\cdot)$ reduces the hidden dimension to 16, and $\text{emb}(t_d, t_i)$ provides a 16-dimensional turn embedding. The 32-dimensional input passes through a 64-unit hidden layer to a scalar gate in $(0, 1)$ via sigmoid. For Qwen-2.5-7B ($L\!=\!28$), this adds ${\sim}$57K parameters ($<$0.001\% of 7B).

\paragraph{Turn embedding.} The embedding $\text{emb}(t_d, t_i)$ encodes turn distance $t_d$ (turns since system prompt) and turn index $t_i$ via learned lookup tables, allowing gates to distinguish conversation positions. The \textbf{Uniform Gate} ablation replaces $\text{emb}(\cdot)$ with a learnable constant, removing turn awareness.

\paragraph{Initialization.} Gate biases are set to 1.5 (${\sim}$82\% pass-through), contrasting ReZero's zero initialization that would suppress pretrained capabilities. Gate parameters receive a $10\times$ learning rate multiplier.

\subsection{Training Objective}\label{sec:loss}

The training objective combines three terms:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_\text{NTP} + \lambda_\text{GAR}\,\mathcal{L}_\text{GAR} + \lambda_\text{div}\,\mathcal{L}_\text{div}.
\end{equation}
$\mathcal{L}_\text{NTP}$ is the standard next-token prediction loss. The GAR loss targets a fixed attention level to goal tokens:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_\text{GAR} = \text{MSE}(\text{GAR\text{-}ours}, \tau),
\end{equation}
where $\tau = 0.5$. GAR is measured at the last token of each assistant turn (GAR-ours, \S\ref{sec:background}). The gate diversity loss prevents collapse to a uniform value:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_\text{div} = \text{ReLU}(\sigma_\text{min} - \text{std}(\mathbf{g})),
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{g} = [g_1, \ldots, g_L]$ and $\sigma_\text{min}$ is a minimum standard deviation threshold. Both $\lambda_\text{GAR} = \lambda_\text{div} = 1.0$.

\paragraph{Loss design evolution.} The MSE formulation was selected from seven iterations spanning four loss families (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-loss}): cumulative (v1--v2) and maximize (v3--v6) objectives either starve gate gradients at equilibrium or drive gates toward identity. Only MSE provides bidirectional pressure---penalizing both over- and under-attention---preventing identity collapse.

\subsection{Controlled Comparison Design}\label{sec:comparison-design}

We design a five-way comparison where each adjacent pair isolates one factor: \textbf{ThreadGate vs.\ Uniform Gate} (turn conditioning), \textbf{ThreadGate vs.\ LoRA-only} (gating mechanism), \textbf{ThreadGate vs.\ +PCGrad} (gradient direction conflict via gradient surgery \citep{yu2020pcgrad}), and \textbf{Fine-tuned vs.\ Base} (LoRA adaptation). All fine-tuned variants share training data, LoRA configuration, optimizer, and seed (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-hyper}).

\section{Training Configuration}\label{sec:appendix-hyper}

Table~\ref{tab:hyperparams} lists the full training configuration shared across all fine-tuned variants. ThreadGate gate parameters receive a $10\times$ learning rate multiplier and are initialized with bias 1.5 ($\sigma(1.5) \approx 0.82$); the gate MLP adds ${\sim}$57K parameters per variant ($<$0.001\% of 7B). The scheduled sampling variant (v8b) linearly increases the model-generated context ratio from 0 to 0.5 over 3 epochs (29.7--37.1\% effective usage due to per-sample Bernoulli sampling). All experiments were conducted on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition GPU (102\,GB VRAM), requiring 6--8 hours per variant.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering\small
\caption{Training hyperparameters for all fine-tuned variants. Entries marked $\dagger$ apply only to ThreadGate and its ablations.}
\label{tab:hyperparams}
\begin{tabular}{l@{\hspace{8pt}}l}
\toprule
\textbf{Parameter} & \textbf{Value} \\
\midrule
Base model & Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct \\
LoRA rank / $\alpha$ & 16 / 32 \\
LoRA targets & \texttt{q\_proj}, \texttt{v\_proj} \\
Optimizer & AdamW ($\beta_1$\!=\!0.9, $\beta_2$\!=\!0.999) \\
Learning rate & $2 \times 10^{-4}$ \\
Gate LR mult.$^\dagger$ & $10\times$ \\
Batch / grad accum & 1 / 8 (eff.\ 8) \\
Epochs & 3 \\
Max seq length & 2048 \\
Training data & 8K UltraChat convs \\
Seed & 42 (+123 for TG, SS) \\
$\lambda_\text{GAR}$/$\lambda_\text{div}$ & 1.0 / 1.0 \\
GAR target $\tau$ & 0.5 \\
Gate init bias$^\dagger$ & 1.5 ($\sigma\!\approx\!0.82$) \\
Gate hidden dim$^\dagger$ & 64 \\
Turn embed dim$^\dagger$ & 16 \\
GPU & RTX PRO 6000 (102\,GB) \\
Training time & 6--8 h / variant \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Loss Formulation Search}\label{sec:appendix-loss}

The final MSE + gate diversity loss (\S\ref{sec:loss}) emerged from seven iterations spanning four loss families. Table~\ref{tab:loss-iterations} summarizes the progression. The search was motivated by a consistent failure mode: naive formulations either collapse to trivial solutions (cumulative losses) or drive gates toward identity (maximize-family losses). We describe each family below.

\paragraph{Cumulative losses (v1--v2).} These losses accumulate GAR contributions across layers, rewarding monotonically increasing attention. v1 collapsed because once the cumulative GAR exceeded the threshold, the loss gradient vanished and gates froze. v2 added an $\varepsilon$-stabilization term to the cumulative product, but numerical instability from the product of 28 per-layer terms dominated, and training followed the same trajectory.

\paragraph{Maximize losses (v3--v5).} These losses directly maximize mean GAR ($-\text{mean}(\text{GAR})$). The unbounded objective rewards full pass-through ($g \to 1.0$), collapsing gate diversity (std $<$ 0.025). At $\lambda$=2 (v3), gates froze within 100 steps; at $\lambda$=10 (v3b), convergence to identity accelerated; at $\lambda$=1 (v5), rebalancing delayed but did not prevent the same degenerate solution.

\paragraph{Smooth saturation (v6).} This bounded the reward using a saturating function, intended to prevent the unbounded growth of maximize losses. In practice, the loss landscape remained monotonic in $g$, and training followed the same terminal trajectory as the maximize family, only more slowly.

\paragraph{MSE + gate diversity (v7).} The MSE formulation provides bidirectional pressure: GAR above $\tau$ incurs the same penalty as GAR below $\tau$, preventing the runaway optimization of maximize losses. The gate diversity regularizer ($\mathcal{L}_\text{div}$) enforces a minimum standard deviation across per-layer gate values, preventing all gates from collapsing to a uniform value. This combination is the only one that achieved stable convergence (GAR-ours = 0.50, PPL = 2.78).

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering\footnotesize
\caption{Loss formulation iterations on Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct. GAR-ours (teacher-forced) and PPL are reported only for the converged configuration (v7).}
\label{tab:loss-iterations}
\begin{tabular}{l l l p{5.8cm} c c}
\toprule
 & \textbf{Loss Family} & \textbf{Status} & \textbf{Failure Mechanism} & \textbf{GAR} & \textbf{PPL} \\
\midrule
